WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.510
- New Zealand has no native
land mammals other than bats.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.388
For around 85 million years,
these islands were so remote

00:00:06.388 --> 00:00:09.720
that the rest of the world's mammals
just never made it here.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.030
All the parts of the ecosystem
that mammals would fill

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.543
instead went to birds,
or lizards, or insects.

00:00:14.543 --> 00:00:16.470
A lot of those birds are flightless,

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.480
because there was nothing
they had to fly away from.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.293
And even the bats spend a
lot of time on the ground.

00:00:21.293 --> 00:00:23.571
Then in the 13th century, Māori arrived

00:00:23.571 --> 00:00:28.218
bringing with them one now-extinct
species of dog, and Polynesian rats.

00:00:28.218 --> 00:00:30.630
That wasn't great for the local species,

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.953
and then in the 18th century,

00:00:31.953 --> 00:00:34.806
Europeans arrived and
brought... a lot.

00:00:34.806 --> 00:00:37.470
Rats, stoats, ferrets,
weasels, possums,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.700
all predators which the local creatures
had not evolved to defend against.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.980
If New Zealand wants to
keep its unique ecosystem

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.604
and wants the local species to recover,

00:00:45.604 --> 00:00:48.423
at least the species that
aren't already extinct,

00:00:48.423 --> 00:00:51.269
then the blunt fact is that
either the local species

00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:53.753
are going to survive,
or the predators are.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.650
The only places in the
world that have managed

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.870
to completely eradicate invasive species

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.153
are very small islands a few
square kilometers across,

00:01:00.153 --> 00:01:03.631
or fenced-off reserves that are
regularly patrolled and studied.

00:01:03.631 --> 00:01:06.600
And most of those are in
and around New Zealand!

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.150
But clearing something
the size of a country,

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.672
and a rural country at that,
that's not just a challenge,

00:01:11.672 --> 00:01:13.288
that's a moonshot.

00:01:13.288 --> 00:01:17.250
So today I met with people
from Predator Free Wellington,

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.276
because they're some of the folks

00:01:18.276 --> 00:01:19.803
who are making a start on it.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.670
- So this is one of
our trusty trap boxes.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.130
They get pretty hammered out here,
with all the elements.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.480
- There's no one in there right now? Okay.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.490
- Hope not.
[both laughing]

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.380
No, it's still set. It's still baited.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:32.213
- Okay.

00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:33.510
- And then we've got a
bait station there too.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:34.710
It's basically toxin.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.470
So this is basically a
very secure plastic box,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.540
it's locked closed so
that pets and children,

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.850
that sort of thing, can't get
access to the bait inside.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.232
And then in here, we've
got a toxic wax block.

00:01:44.232 --> 00:01:46.110
- Oh, you're just touching that.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:46.943
- Yup.

00:01:46.943 --> 00:01:47.910
[both laughing]

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.940
- Well, people or even cats and dogs

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.710
would have to eat a
huge quantity of this

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.600
for it to have any effects, whereas a rat

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.601
only needs really five grams
or so for a lethal dose.

00:01:57.601 --> 00:01:58.434
- Oh, wow.

00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:00.630
- So it makes it a really effective tool.
There's also an antidote.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.520
- And that is presumably
delicious to rats.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.500
- Yeah, they tend to love it.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.360
I don't know what the flavor is in there,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.618
it's a cereal-based bait, basically.

00:02:07.618 --> 00:02:08.451
- Right.

00:02:08.451 --> 00:02:11.092
- And then this other one
here is kind of interesting.

00:02:11.092 --> 00:02:13.436
- We'll wait for the plane.

00:02:13.436 --> 00:02:16.530
[plane engine roaring]

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:17.670
Right next to the airport.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.720
- This is called Detex,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.881
so it's a non-toxic type of
bait and rats love to eat it,

00:02:21.881 --> 00:02:24.180
but the reason we use it is because

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.280
it actually glows under
UV light, bright green.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.530
And so they'll eat this
and then their droppings

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.170
or their urine or even
their insides will glow.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.240
And so for us and our barrier
system, it means that

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.273
if we detect a rat along here
somewhere,

00:02:35.273 --> 00:02:36.299
or we catch one in a trap,

00:02:36.299 --> 00:02:38.810
we can work out where
it's come from, basically.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.982
- Right. So that peninsula
is free of rats.

00:02:41.982 --> 00:02:43.440
- Yep. Just about.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:44.670
- That section isn't.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:45.503
- Yup.

00:02:45.503 --> 00:02:48.900
- And the buffer is, right now,
the airport and this bridge here.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.690
- Yeah. So the airport provides a
really nice natural buffer area

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.970
because rats don't
particularly like to be out

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.620
in the open where it's super exposed.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.000
And so we don't see much evidence

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.621
of them crossing that
big concrete expanse.

00:02:59.621 --> 00:03:03.540
- So the plan is: start at the
edge of the Miramar Peninsula,

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.740
eradicate the predators there

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.360
and then establish a buffer zone,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.826
and then keep pushing
that buffer zone back

00:03:07.826 --> 00:03:09.450
and back and back and back

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.760
until eventually there
are no predators left

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.530
in the whole of Wellington.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.830
- We've got about 5,800
traps in our network

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.308
and then about 6,800 bait stations
of varying types.

00:03:20.308 --> 00:03:23.670
- The trap to me just looks
like a box with a hole in it.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:24.503
- Exactly.

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:26.310
- And, if the rat can get in,
how does the rat not get out?

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.750
- It's basically an entrance on one side

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.610
and the mesh at the other
side they can see through,

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.004
so it looks like a clear run for them.

00:03:32.004 --> 00:03:33.840
Which we've found
makes them more likely to

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.310
go inside and investigate.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.267
And then we've got some
lure inside as well.

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:40.077
So there's a paste that
just smells really good

00:03:40.077 --> 00:03:42.263
and so they'll go in for a nosey.

00:03:42.263 --> 00:03:45.090
I can whip this open, there we go.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.740
- Oh, okay. Yeah.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:47.910
- So it's a pretty grunty trap.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.160
- Yeah. In my head, up until this moment,

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.020
I was like, oh, it's a trap.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:53.855
The rat goes in, the rat gets trapped,
you release the rat...

00:03:53.855 --> 00:03:55.500
No, you don't.
- Yeah, no.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.623
- No. That...
- No. This is definitely a lethal trap.

00:03:56.623 --> 00:03:57.960
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.330
And it's humane certified to
the New Zealand standards.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.240
So we basically want traps
that the animal's not

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.500
going to suffer more
than it needs to.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.110
It's going to be pretty
instant, pretty powerful.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.630
I can give you a demo of
setting it off if you'd like.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:10.566
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:10.566 --> 00:04:15.478
I mean, it's kind of really
brutal calculus, isn't it?

00:04:15.478 --> 00:04:19.290
Either the rat dies or the
unique species here dies.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.490
- Unfortunately. Yeah.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.320
I mean, it's never nice
to have to kill animals

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.970
and rats, I've got a
lot of respect for them,

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.380
they're great creatures
in their own right,

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.863
but unfortunately they just can't coexist
with our native species.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.290
So, I can give this a demo.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.850
- On that note. Yeah, let's...

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:33.840
- So I'm just going to use my gloves

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.580
'cos they're nice and soft,
and lob them in

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.245
and hopefully that will set it off.

00:04:37.245 --> 00:04:40.410
Two, one. [trap clanks]

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:41.243
There we go.

00:04:41.243 --> 00:04:42.788
- Okay. Yeah.

00:04:42.788 --> 00:04:44.280
- So it's pretty punchy.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:45.210
- That's going to be messy.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.200
- Yeah. - Yeah.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:47.880
How often do you have to
clean one of these out?

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.830
- Not very much these days, thankfully.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:50.663
- Yeah.

00:04:50.663 --> 00:04:52.685
- Along here it's still
reasonably rare but we do catch

00:04:52.685 --> 00:04:56.544
probably about three rats each
time we service this barrier system.

00:04:56.544 --> 00:04:57.930
On the peninsula,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.970
we're catching a lot less
rats now, which is nice.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:01.976
Yeah. Especially in this hot weather.

00:05:01.976 --> 00:05:03.750
- You really do have to be so careful.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:04.650
- Oh, yeah.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.240
- How many people do you
have working on this?

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.030
- So we've got a full-time
field staff of about 25,

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.650
and then there's a few of us
in the office as well,

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.540
doing the data crunching
and that sort of thing.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:16.950
- That's way more than I thought.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.470
- And then on top of that, we've
got a huge volunteer force.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.740
So they make up about 10%
of the efforts on Miramar Peninsula.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.380
- Right. And it's government
funded, presumably?

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.260
- Yeah, a lot of it is.
So it's a big collaboration, this one.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.535
So, what we can see here, we've had a rat,

00:05:31.535 --> 00:05:34.830
come along and chewed this
monitoring card of ours.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:36.387
- So we call these chew cards.

00:05:36.387 --> 00:05:38.790
They're a really effective
monitoring tool.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.640
It's basically just a
piece of corflute plastic

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:45.400
and then we mash peanut
butter into the gaps.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.670
- But it works a treat.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.530
So it's been here, within
the last week we know,

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.390
because we've serviced
this quite recently.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:52.879
- You can't poison the peanut butter then?

00:05:52.879 --> 00:05:54.580
- Unfortunately not.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:57.411
We considered the avenue,
but I don't think the...

00:05:57.411 --> 00:05:58.244
- Of course,

00:05:58.244 --> 00:06:00.023
because that's something
that other creatures...

00:06:00.023 --> 00:06:01.560
- Other creatures could get to.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.177
A dog could have a nibble.

00:06:03.177 --> 00:06:05.550
And it's also the combining
of toxin with food

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:06.538
is one of those things that...

00:06:06.538 --> 00:06:08.970
- Oh. - ...I don't think you're
technically allowed to do.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.400
So, we've got to be very
careful to keep it all safe

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.320
and legal and all of that.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:14.610
- No, the trap has not been set off now.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:15.480
- The trap's not been set.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.760
So it's had a nibble,
it's getting interested,

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.108
but it hasn't gone in yet.

00:06:19.108 --> 00:06:21.300
- Yet. [both laughing]

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.031
- The trouble is, as long as there's
a breeding pair of rats

00:06:24.031 --> 00:06:27.270
anywhere outside that
buffer zone, there's a threat.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:29.370
Alberta in Canada is the only place

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.420
outside of the poles
where there are no rats,

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.080
and that's because rats
never got established there.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.090
A combination of harsh geography
on all sides

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:39.911
and a vicious, almost obsessive
government-backed control program

00:06:39.911 --> 00:06:42.906
kept rats out, and
still does to this day.

00:06:42.906 --> 00:06:46.590
That's what Wellington will
always have to deal with,

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.593
unless they just keep pushing.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:53.761
The reserve here, this is where
the rats have been eliminated

00:06:53.761 --> 00:06:55.200
rather than suppressed, right?

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.033
- Yeah, exactly.

00:06:56.033 --> 00:07:00.059
So it took us about six months
of really intense efforts,

00:07:00.059 --> 00:07:03.450
but we've now eliminated all
of the rats out of this area.

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.160
- So how do you know?

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.350
- We've got a lot of
monitoring that goes into it,

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:08.970
trail cameras, a big one.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.310
They're just about the
golden standard we can get

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:13.200
for monitoring rats.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.540
You can see there's one
just tucked down in here.

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:18.570
- Oh, spot the city boy.
[both laughing]

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:20.130
Oh, okay. Yep.

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:21.810
- So they're camouflaged to blend in

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:24.000
as best they can, so that
the rats don't notice them

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.590
and it's pointed at a motor lure.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:28.482
It's basically got a
syringe of mayonnaise inside

00:07:28.482 --> 00:07:30.360
with a little motor.
- Sure, okay.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.451
- And once a day, it
basically pushes out 1ml of

00:07:33.451 --> 00:07:35.310
mayonnaise from this syringe.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.230
Without us having to come
here every single day,

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:40.290
it's keeping a fresh food source
and a fresh lure for these rats.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.801
- And anything that comes to eat it...

00:07:41.801 --> 00:07:43.320
- Gets caught on camera.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:44.153
- Right.

00:07:44.153 --> 00:07:45.960
- Yeah. It's a really
effective way of basically

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.910
telling us if there's
any rats left in an area.

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:49.950
And rats seem to love egg mayonnaise,

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.570
so it works pretty well.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:52.890
- And are there any left?

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:53.723
- Not in here.

00:07:53.723 --> 00:07:56.940
It's been a huge effort, but now
we can safely say it's rat free.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:58.020
- Is it working?

00:07:58.020 --> 00:07:59.790
Well, it seems to. The statistics say

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.550
that animal life is
recovering on the peninsula.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.950
The key thing is, this isn't
limited to just Wellington,

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.100
across the whole of New Zealand
there are groups doing the same.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.682
There's a government plan
called Predator Free 2050,

00:08:10.682 --> 00:08:13.290
but it is optimistic.
The plan that even admits

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.030
that to pull this off on a national scale

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:17.820
they'll need some new scientific discovery
to make this easier.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:21.540
And "new scientific discoveries"
and "ecosystem management"

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:24.720
are words that historically
haven't gone well together.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:25.860
Do you think it's going to work?

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.010
Do you think that they'll get
New Zealand clear by 2050?

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.180
- I think they will, yeah.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:31.980
I think just looking at
the progress we've made

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.380
in the last couple of years
already, particularly here,

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.230
we've learnt so much
in the last 12 months

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:38.827
that if you extrapolate that out,

00:08:38.827 --> 00:08:40.830
I think we've definitely
got a good shot of it.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:42.393
I think we'll crack it.

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:44.290
- Can it be done?

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:48.270
Maybe. With enough money,
time and human effort.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:49.710
And from my limited time here

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:52.380
I'd say that protection
of the land and ecosystem,

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.108
the Māori word roughly
translated is 'kaitiakitanga',

00:08:55.108 --> 00:08:58.059
it seems to be part of
New Zealand's national identity,

00:08:58.059 --> 00:09:02.070
in a way I don't think I've seen
in any other country I've been to.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.560
Towns, regions, sure. But never a country.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.833
If anyone can pull this off,
it's going to be New Zealand.

